@KameraShy There have been a couple legal disputes leading to decisions by the Copyright Office, notably the "monkey selfie copyright dispute", wherein USCO 'stated that works that lack human authorship, such as "a photograph taken by a monkey", cannot have their copyright registered at the US Copyright Office'.  Policy is that if a human didn't participate directly in the creation (like pressing the button to take the photo), the work cannot be copyrighted at all, it is public domain.

The photographer intentionally orchestrated the monkey stealing the camera and taking the selfie and he was denied copyright. PETA tried to get the monkey to own the copyright and USCO said no, no one gets copyright.

That set the stage.. more recently someone created an entire graphic novel using AI-generated images and copyright office granted them copyright on the text and the prompts, which they wrote, but not the images which it stated cannot be coyrighted as they were not produced by a human.

