@andywest5773 I am assuming nothing....I have been a developer or team lead for over 25 years now.     The 3 engineers was an extreme example to make the math obvious.   

If you don't believe AI can make software engineers more efficient, you either haven't tried the latest tools or need to be more detailed in your requirements and prompts.  


It doesn't work for every use case, but a huge amount of software development is about to require a lot less effort.  Either prepare for it or be left behind.

@WblutSundAehre Then you either aren't writing detailed enough prompts or you aren't using the right tools.  

I am also a senior SWE / team lead for a global SAAS company with multiple web based UIs.   We are seeing a huge decrease in delivery times across our organization.   Everyting is stillcode reviewed and QA reviewed for technical quality, but output is up significantly (~20%-40% depending on how much you trust story points as an accurate representation of effort).  

However, it does take a completely different management-driven attitude towards AI development.   Developers still need to review and correct code before submitting it for review.   Developers should also be trained on the proper use of models and how to write prompts that promote technical accuracy.   It isn't an easy path, but if the big AI providers can make the economics work, this will heavily impact how we write software.

@leo-g3j2l I’d argue the core idea is very different. Compilers used languages with consistent semantics and meaning which are interpreted in logically tractable ways.

Natural language isn’t that and statistical representations of natural language is even further removed.

The only similarity between compilers and LLMs is that they’re text to text converters.

@And@AndrewEddie good engineers would use the least amount of code to solve a problem. I believe that’s true even nowadays.

Bad engineers ship the first thing that appears to work. That is now automated at scale.

There will be so much more bad code than good code it’ll make the current state of software look like a golden age

@AndrewEddie agile isn’t the best fit for all projects, though.

It was designed for contracting firms with clients who actually know what they want a product to be.

It’s a poor fit for exploration and maintenance

@AndrewEddie knowing what you want doesn’t mean waterfall.

Agile doesn’t mean starting with no plan in mind….

Agile requires some kind of stakeholder to be able to identify if a project is on the right track in order to provide steering feedback at the end of each sprint. So they need to know what they want, even if they don’t know every detail.

@bosslogq A lot depends on the complexity of the application and the complexity of individual features. I initially generate around 90% with AI. Generating 100% does not make sense, because it is faster to write business rules manually from the start. However, Codex App with GPT5.5 makes a lot of mistakes. It generates things that are different from what I meant in my prompt, and AI also produces redundancy, bugs, vulnerabilities, and non-optimal code. And of course, there is crapcode, a lot of crapcode. Therefore, during the review, I preparing corrective prompts for fixing, while I handle small issues manually on the fly. This is how 90% of the generated code becoming 70%, or maybe even 60%.

In any case, this is regular programming. It is like the transition from C to C#. I use natural language as a programming language. Unlike with C#, I also need to be able to read code quickly and fix it manually. When I wrote in C# without AI, I did not spend time reading CIL. Now, AI is not able to translate my intentions into C# correctly. In almost 100% of cases, I need to make edits either through corrective prompts or manually.

Also, in my opinion, AI has not really become smarter over the last 2 years. Programmers have written thousands of lines for agents that brute-force syntax errors, collect context for LLM, and so on. But the logic of AI decisions is the same as it was in the GPT4o model.

