@one4runner435 Agree, the clock is ticking for sure and its advancing rapidly, but at the same time its extremely expensive. I don't think any AI company is making any profit right now, we just saw OpenAI cancel Sora, so its already starting. 

I suspect we might hit a plateau with more jr level jobs being lost, but senior devs who know how to use AI efficiently and effectively will continue to be of value, with advancement halting until a more cost efficient method for AI is found. I do think there is a limit until that's solved. For example, right now having AI write a normal email equates to running a microwave on high for 1 minute energy wise, not to mention the water cost for cooling. 

So I think 10-20 years like you said is accurate, but that could be longer or shorter all coming down to making it more efficient. A lot of people have tried so far, but the way it works right now I don't think any advancements will happen soon, but who knows.
